// pfodWeb NOTE (button/LED/NeoPixel tagging + dedicated-hardware pin
// exclusion audit, 2026-07-13):
//
// Board: Adafruit MacroPad RP2040 (PID 5100) - a real 12-key mechanical
// keyboard/macro-pad device with a rotary encoder (with integrated
// push-button), a 128x64 monochrome OLED display on dedicated hardware
// SPI, and a 3x4 grid of 12 NeoPixels (one per key).
//
// Sources consulted (WebFetch/WebSearch, 2026-07-13):
//  1. https://learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-macropad-rp2040/pinouts
//     (authoritative Arduino/CircuitPython pin-name reference)
//  2. https://learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-macropad-rp2040/macropad-basics
//  3. https://github.com/adafruit/Adafruit_CircuitPython_MacroPad/blob/main/adafruit_macropad.py
//     (confirms LED polarity via the library's own digitalio usage)
//  4. This board's own variants/rp2040/rp2040/adafruit_macropad2040/pins_arduino.h
//     (bespoke, not ../generic_full/common.h - see its own pfodWeb NOTE)
//
// KEYS (GPIO1-12, board.KEY1..KEY12 / Arduino pins 1-12): confirmed via
// source #1 - "The sockets are available in CircuitPython as board.KEY1
// through board.KEY12. In Arduino they are pins 1 through 12" and
// "Pressing a key grounds the pin, so set a pull-up on each pin." These
// are 12 genuine individually-wired mechanical key switches (not a
// row/column matrix). This board's own pins_arduino.h already declares
// D1-D12 static-const aliases for GPIO1-12, so they reach the parser
// with full chip-default capabilities (digital_input/digital_output/
// pwm_output) unless overridden - overridden below to
// ["digital_input","button"].
//
// ENCODER SWITCH (GPIO0, PIN_SWITCH): confirmed via source #1 - "The
// BOOT button is available in CircuitPython at board.ENCODER_SWITCH or
// board.BUTTON. It is available in Arduino at PIN_SWITCH. Pressing the
// button grounds the pin." This is the rotary encoder's integrated
// push-button (press down on the knob), which doubles as a BOOT-mode
// button when held at power-up. GPIO0 has no static-const alias in this
// board's pins_arduino.h (grouped under its own "Extra hardware!"
// comment, no D0 declared), so it is invisible to the parser without an
// explicit chipGpios entry - added below as ["digital_input","button"]
// so it surfaces correctly rather than being silently absent.
//
// ENCODER A/B (GPIO18=PIN_ROTA, GPIO17=PIN_ROTB): confirmed via source
// #1 as the rotary encoder's quadrature lines (CircuitPython
// board.ROTA/ENCODER_A, board.ROTB/ENCODER_B). Neither has a
// static-const alias in this board's pins_arduino.h (no D17/D18
// declared), so both are already absent/excluded from the parser's pin
// list by default. Left fully excluded here (explicit capabilities: []
// entries added purely for documentation, matching this repo's
// established precedent of excluding rotary-encoder quadrature pins
// entirely - see esp32s3/m5stack_dinmeter's "GPIO40/41 (rotary encoder
// quadrature)" exclusion). No "encoder"/"quadrature" capability tag
// exists in this repo's vocabulary, and these pins are permanently
// claimed by the onboard encoder hardware, so they are not offered as
// general-purpose digital I/O.
//
// LED (GPIO13, PIN_LED): confirmed via source #1 - "The red LED is
// available in CircuitPython at board.LED. It is available in Arduino
// at PIN_LED" (a single red status LED next to the USB port, separate
// from the 12 NeoPixels). Polarity confirmed active-high via source #3
// - Adafruit's own CircuitPython MacroPad helper library wraps board.LED
// as `self._led = digitalio.DigitalInOut(board.LED)` and exposes it as
// `red_led`, where `macropad.red_led = True` turns the LED on (library
// docstring: "The red led next to the USB port" with True/False
// setter directly driving `.value`). Tagged
// ["digital_output","pwm_output","led_high"], matching this repo's
// established led_high precedent (rpipico, akana_r1, wiznet_5100s_evb_pico).
//
// NEOPIXELS (GPIO19, PIN_NEOPIXEL, NUM_NEOPIXEL=12): confirmed via
// source #1 - "The NeoPixel LEDs are available in CircuitPython as
// board.NEOPIXEL. They are available in Arduino as PIN_NEOPIXEL," with
// the page describing the onboard 3x4 (12-pixel) grid, matching this
// board's own NUM_NEOPIXEL=12 #define. Tagged
// ["digital_output","led_neopixel","pwm_output"], matching this repo's
// established led_neopixel precedent (sparkfun_thingplusrp2350 GPIO14).
//
// SPEAKER (GPIO16=PIN_SPEAKER, GPIO14=PIN_SPEAKER_ENABLE): confirmed
// via source #1 as the onboard piezo speaker's PWM audio-output pin and
// its dedicated amplifier-enable pin (CircuitPython board.SPEAKER /
// board.SPEAKER_ENABLE). Neither has a static-const alias in this
// board's pins_arduino.h (no D14/D16 declared - already grouped under
// the header's own "Extra hardware!" comment), so both are already
// absent from the parser's pin list by default. Explicit
// capabilities: [] entries added below purely for documentation,
// matching this repo's established dedicated-onboard-peripheral
// exclusion precedent (e.g. wiznet_5500_evb_pico2's Ethernet SPI/reset/
// interrupt lines).
//
// OLED DISPLAY (GPIO22=OLED_CS, GPIO23=OLED_RST, GPIO24=OLED_DC, plus
// the SPI1 bus GPIO26=SCK/GPIO27=MOSI/GPIO28=MISO): confirmed via
// source #1 - "on high-speed hardware SPI to ensure quick updates" -
// this board's onboard 128x64 monochrome OLED. OLED_CS/OLED_RST/OLED_DC
// are plain #defines with no static-const alias (no D22/D23/D24
// declared), so they are already absent from the parser's pin list by
// default; explicit capabilities: [] entries added below purely for
// documentation. GPIO26/27/28, by contrast, DO carry static-const
// aliases in this board's pins_arduino.h (D26/D27/D28, doubling as
// A0/A1/A2) even though the same header separately declares them as
// PIN_SPI1_SCK/MOSI/MISO for the OLED's dedicated hardware SPI bus -
// this is the exact alias-collision gotcha flagged for this audit
// (a confirmed dedicated-hardware pin also carrying a general D<n>/A<n>
// alias that would otherwise expose it as ordinary digital/analog I/O).
// Unlike the adafruit_feather_scorpio precedent, this SPI1 bus is
// genuinely in active use (driving the onboard OLED), not a spare/
// unused bus, so the fix here is a chipGpios capability override
// (capabilities: []) rather than deleting the #define. GPIO28 (MISO) is
// included even though a typical write-only OLED module has no SDO/MISO
// line physically bonded, because arduino-pico's SPI1 peripheral claims
// the GPIO28 pin-mux function for its hardware SPI role whenever the
// display library initializes SPI1, making it unavailable as ordinary
// digital I/O in practice - same reasoning as the wiznet_*_evb_pico*
// boards' uniform MISO/MOSI/SCK/SS exclusion for their onboard Ethernet
// SPI bus.
//
// GPIO15, GPIO20/21 (PIN_WIRE0_SDA/SCL - STEMMA QT I2C connector),
// GPIO25, GPIO29 (A3): NO CHANGE. GPIO15/25 are ordinary spare GPIOs
// with a D-alias and no other declared use. GPIO20/21 are the genuine
// user-facing STEMMA QT I2C expansion connector (confirmed via source
// #1: "you can use the STEMMA connector with board.SCL and board.SDA"),
// not an internally-dedicated bus, so left at full default capability
// (i2c_sda/i2c_scl auto-tagging is out of scope for this audit and does
// not fire here anyway, since this board's bespoke pins_arduino.h
// deliberately declares no unqualified SDA/SCL static-const aliases).
// GPIO29/A3 is a genuine spare analog-capable pin with no other
// declared use.
//
// Full findings/sources trail:
// boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/adafruit_macropad2040/notes.txt
{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2040" -> designer/boards/RP2040/RP2040.json -> const RP2040Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2040",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI.  Overridden
  // by the matching `<id>.name=` entry in a sibling-or-ancestor
  // boards.txt when one is present (matched via `<id>.build.variant=`
  // against this variant's directory name).
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040 board - see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  // This board's buttons (12 keys + encoder switch) are ordinary GPIO
  // push-buttons ("Pressing a key/the button grounds the pin" per
  // Adafruit's own docs) with a software pull-up, not capacitive-touch
  // or otherwise input-only - handled entirely via the chipGpios
  // "button" tags below, so no inputOnlyGpios entry is needed for them.
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports - Serial only.
  "connections": ["serial"],

  "pinNotes": {},

  "chipGpios": {
    "0":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "PIN_SWITCH - rotary encoder's integrated push-button switch (press down on the knob), also doubles as the BOOT-mode button when held at power-up. Confirmed via learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-macropad-rp2040/pinouts: \"The BOOT button is available in CircuitPython at board.ENCODER_SWITCH or board.BUTTON. It is available in Arduino at PIN_SWITCH. Pressing the button grounds the pin.\" No static-const alias in this board's pins_arduino.h, so this entry is required for the pin to surface at all." },
    "1":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY1 - one of 12 individually-wired mechanical key switches (D1 static-const alias, Arduino pin 1). Confirmed via learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-macropad-rp2040/pinouts: \"The sockets are available in CircuitPython as board.KEY1 through board.KEY12. In Arduino they are pins 1 through 12... Pressing a key grounds the pin.\"" },
    "2":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY2 - see GPIO1 (KEY1) note above; same source, same confirmation." },
    "3":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY3 - see GPIO1 (KEY1) note above; same source, same confirmation." },
    "4":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY4 - see GPIO1 (KEY1) note above; same source, same confirmation." },
    "5":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY5 - see GPIO1 (KEY1) note above; same source, same confirmation." },
    "6":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY6 - see GPIO1 (KEY1) note above; same source, same confirmation." },
    "7":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY7 - see GPIO1 (KEY1) note above; same source, same confirmation." },
    "8":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY8 - see GPIO1 (KEY1) note above; same source, same confirmation." },
    "9":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY9 - see GPIO1 (KEY1) note above; same source, same confirmation." },
    "10": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY10 - see GPIO1 (KEY1) note above; same source, same confirmation." },
    "11": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY11 - see GPIO1 (KEY1) note above; same source, same confirmation." },
    "12": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "KEY12 - see GPIO1 (KEY1) note above; same source, same confirmation." },
    "13": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "pwm_output", "led_high"], "note": "PIN_LED - onboard red status LED next to the USB port (distinct from the 12 NeoPixels). Confirmed active-high via Adafruit's own Adafruit_CircuitPython_MacroPad library (adafruit_macropad.py): wraps board.LED as a DigitalInOut and exposes red_led such that `macropad.red_led = True` turns it on." },
    "14": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPEAKER_ENABLE - dedicated enable line for the onboard piezo speaker's amplifier, not general purpose. Confirmed via learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-macropad-rp2040/pinouts (board.SPEAKER_ENABLE / PIN_SPEAKER_ENABLE). No static-const alias in this board's pins_arduino.h; entry added for documentation." },
    "16": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPEAKER - dedicated PWM audio-output line to the onboard piezo speaker, not general purpose. Confirmed via learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-macropad-rp2040/pinouts (board.SPEAKER / PIN_SPEAKER). No static-const alias in this board's pins_arduino.h; entry added for documentation." },
    "17": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_ROTB - rotary encoder quadrature channel B, permanently dedicated to the onboard encoder, not general purpose. Confirmed via learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-macropad-rp2040/pinouts (board.ROTB / board.ENCODER_B). No static-const alias in this board's pins_arduino.h; entry added for documentation, matching this repo's rotary-encoder-quadrature exclusion precedent (esp32s3/m5stack_dinmeter GPIO40/41)." },
    "18": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_ROTA - rotary encoder quadrature channel A, permanently dedicated to the onboard encoder, not general purpose. Confirmed via learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-macropad-rp2040/pinouts (board.ROTA / board.ENCODER_A). No static-const alias in this board's pins_arduino.h; entry added for documentation, matching this repo's rotary-encoder-quadrature exclusion precedent (esp32s3/m5stack_dinmeter GPIO40/41)." },
    "19": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "led_neopixel", "pwm_output"], "note": "PIN_NEOPIXEL - onboard 3x4 grid of 12 addressable NeoPixels (NUM_NEOPIXEL=12), one per key. Confirmed via learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-macropad-rp2040/pinouts: \"The NeoPixel LEDs are available in CircuitPython as board.NEOPIXEL. They are available in Arduino as PIN_NEOPIXEL.\"" },
    "22": { "capabilities": [], "note": "OLED_CS - dedicated chip-select line to the onboard 128x64 monochrome OLED's hardware SPI1 bus, not general purpose. Confirmed via learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-macropad-rp2040/pinouts (\"on high-speed hardware SPI\"). No static-const alias in this board's pins_arduino.h; entry added for documentation." },
    "23": { "capabilities": [], "note": "OLED_RST - dedicated reset line to the onboard OLED, not general purpose. See GPIO22 (OLED_CS) note above for sourcing. No static-const alias in this board's pins_arduino.h; entry added for documentation." },
    "24": { "capabilities": [], "note": "OLED_DC - dedicated data/command select line to the onboard OLED, not general purpose. See GPIO22 (OLED_CS) note above for sourcing. No static-const alias in this board's pins_arduino.h; entry added for documentation." },
    "26": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPI1_SCK - dedicated hardware-SPI clock line to the onboard OLED. This board's pins_arduino.h also declares this GPIO as D26/A0 (general digital/analog aliases) despite it being consumed by the OLED's active SPI1 bus - flagged alias-collision gotcha for this audit; excluded here rather than editing the header, since the bus is genuinely in use (unlike the adafruit_feather_scorpio precedent's spare/unused SS default). See GPIO22 (OLED_CS) note above for sourcing." },
    "27": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPI1_MOSI - dedicated hardware-SPI data line to the onboard OLED. Also declared as D27/A1 in this board's pins_arduino.h despite active OLED use - see GPIO26 (PIN_SPI1_SCK) note above for the same alias-collision reasoning." },
    "28": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SPI1_MISO - part of the onboard OLED's dedicated hardware SPI1 bus. A write-only OLED module typically has no physical SDO/MISO line bonded, but arduino-pico's SPI1 peripheral still claims this GPIO's pin-mux function whenever the display library initializes SPI1, making it unavailable for ordinary digital I/O in practice (same reasoning as the wiznet_*_evb_pico* boards' uniform SPI-bus exclusion). Also declared as D28/A2 in this board's pins_arduino.h - see GPIO26 (PIN_SPI1_SCK) note above for the alias-collision reasoning." }
  },

  // Same 8-bit analogWrite() default as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // 4 analog channels (A0-A3 = GPIO26-29), 12-bit ADC - matches the
  // chip-level default. Note A0-A2 (GPIO26-28) are consumed by the
  // onboard OLED's SPI1 bus (see chipGpios above) and so are not
  // actually usable as analog inputs on this board; only A3 (GPIO29) is
  // genuinely free.
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
